const HTML = "<p>I was fifteen when the doctor told me I had cancer.</p>\n<p>I watched his mouth form the word and waited to feel something. I understood what he was saying, knew that people died of cancer, but he might as well have been reading out a McDonald’s menu. What I remember most is how strange it was to feel so little.</p>\n<p>My parents were taking in the news while I sat there. I don’t know whether being young helped or whether what he’d said simply hadn’t sunk in. I didn’t cry, and I don’t remember asking whether I would die.</p>\n<p>Until then, I’d had very little reason to feel unsafe. My father was someone I could depend on, and I took that for granted. He didn’t need to tell me he was strong; if he gave his word, I trusted him to keep it. My mother made home a place I wanted to return to. I remember coffee in the afternoon, music while dinner cooked, her hand on my shoulder as she passed. I could go out into the world, try something, fail and come home, without ever really considering that any of this might end.</p>\n<p>Within days I was in hospital with tubes taped to my skin. I remember a doctor explaining that they would calculate what would kill me, then reduce it a little. Whatever else I understood, I knew the treatment was going to hurt me too.</p>\n<p>Life settled into three-week cycles: a week of treatment, a week when I couldn’t eat, then a week of eating as much as I could before we started again. Food I’d once liked could make me feel sick from the smell alone, and sometimes I struggled even with water. Then my appetite would return and I’d put a little weight back on, until I began to feel more like myself and it was time to go back to hospital.</p>\n<p>I wasn’t spending much time thinking about death. Sometimes getting through the next hour was enough to keep me occupied, and what I could manage to eat mattered more than what might happen to me later.</p>\n<p>There were other children on the ward, some much younger than me. I’d see them in the playroom with their IV stands, though I rarely spoke to them. I think I was ashamed of being ill, even if I couldn’t have explained why. I didn’t want people looking at me without my hair, weak and attached to a machine, so I kept to myself.</p>\n<p>Some children died.</p>\n<p>I’d come back and find a bed empty, with fresh sheets and no parent sitting beside it. I knew what had happened, but I couldn’t stay with the thought for long. The child was gone, and thinking about that meant thinking about what might happen to me. I’d look away.</p>\n<p>My friends still had school, crushes, arguments and parties. I don’t blame them for that; they were my age too. But I was learning which blood counts had to improve before I could go home, and the children on the ward made it impossible to keep thinking of death as something that happened much later in life.</p>\n<p>I survived.</p>\n<p>My hair grew back and I returned to school, glad that people could once again expect something more of me than getting through treatment.</p>\n<p>My recovery didn’t mean we could all return to the life we’d had before. My parents loved me and loved each other, but my illness had put their marriage under strain. They were frightened and exhausted, conversations were sharper, and the silences between them lasted longer. Even after I was well, home didn’t feel as safe as I remembered.</p>\n<p>More people died in the years that followed, and I found myself in hospital corridors again, this time beside someone else’s bed. I’d learnt to hold still for needles and swallow pills, but when someone I loved was lying in the bed, there was so little I could do. Adjust a blanket, offer some water, touch an arm. I was physically well and strong, and none of that could make them better.</p>\n<p>I began thinking about my own death at night. It wasn’t a particular pleasure or ambition I couldn’t bear to lose, but all of it: the memories, the people I loved, the private jokes that needed no explanation because the right person was there. The world would carry on, with people buying groceries and complaining about traffic, and I wouldn’t be in it. That was perfectly reasonable, and I resented it.</p>\n<p>Whenever I tried to imagine being absent, I somehow remained there, aware of everything I was missing. I’d picture nonexistence as though it were a place where I would be left alone, knowing that everyone else was still alive. I could frighten myself very effectively with that picture.</p>\n<p>Religion didn’t give me an answer I could be sure of, and philosophy gave me plenty to think about without giving me any way to know what happened after death. I kept trying to imagine something I had no experience of.</p>\n<p>But if consciousness ends when I die, there won’t be anyone left to miss being alive. I won’t be somewhere wishing I could come back, because the part of me capable of wishing will have ended too.</p>\n<p>I understood that long before I found much comfort in it. The fear was happening now, while I was alive, and understanding why the picture made no sense didn’t make it stop frightening me. Over time, though, it helped. I became less convinced that I was going to be shut out of life and left to suffer because of it.</p>\n<p>I’d also been thinking almost entirely about what I wanted to keep, while leaving out everything I’d be glad to be free of: pain, anxiety, regret, a memory I wished would stop coming back. If death ends experience, it ends those things too. It made no sense to imagine losing every pleasure while somehow keeping the ability to suffer.</p>\n<p>I wouldn’t offer any of this to the parents of a child who had died. Those children should have had the time I’ve had. I wouldn’t tell their parents to regard the loss as merciful, and I wouldn’t have wanted anyone to say it to mine. I’m describing how I’ve come to think about my own eventual death, after years of finding the thought almost impossible to bear.</p>\n<p>I still want to live. I expect that when I’m dying I’ll want more time, and I may be frightened regardless of what I’ve worked out while healthy. Understanding something in a quiet room is not the same as living through it in a hospital bed.</p>\n<p>But death no longer hits the same. If there is something beyond it, I don&#39;t know what it is; if there is nothing, I won&#39;t be there to regret anything. If there&#39;s something, then I know I can stand by my choices. Regardless, I know that when that time comes, my question will be finally answered, or finally forgotten.</p>\n";

